WEBVTT

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:31.973
That's another kind of authenticity,

00:08:31.997 --> 00:08:34.457
being blunt with your opinions,

00:08:34.481 --> 00:08:37.060
which you see an awful lot
on the internet.

00:08:37.084 --> 00:08:40.036
People seem to feel like
they can say nearly anything online

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:42.823
under the guise of,
"I'm just being authentic."

00:08:42.847 --> 00:08:45.907
But what happens when
that kind of online commentary

00:08:45.931 --> 00:08:47.102
is your job?

00:08:47.126 --> 00:08:51.246
Leah Finnegan: There is one
specific incident that kind of haunts me.

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:53.346
AG: Leah Finnegan is a journalist.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.936
In 2015, she was working at "Gawker,"

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:59.296
the notoriously irreverent,
gossipy website.

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:01.936
It was a place where
raw self-expression online